            %p
              In a more general abstract discussion than we present here, one finds that by applying a projective resolution functor
              = succeed "," do
                %em on chain complexes
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphic
              to the one obtained by the above composite.
            %p
              This means that if one is only interested in the “weak chain homology type” of the chain complex in the image of a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain homology
              groups of the chain complexes in the images of the above composite gives the desired information. This is what def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RightDerivedFunctorOfLeftExactFunctor"}
              and def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LeftDerivedFunctorOfRightExactFunctor"}
              below do.
          #LeftRightExactFunctor.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
